.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt BWRAP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bwrap
.Nd run a program in new namespaces, as a sandbox
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm ,
bubblewrap, makes a mount namespace and the other namespaces its options
name, builds a new root in it from the mounts the options describe, and runs
.Ar command
there.
.Fl -bind Ar src dest
and
.Fl -ro-bind Ar src dest
show a file or directory of the old root in the new one, writable or
read-only;
.Fl -proc Ar dest ,
.Fl -dev Ar dest
and
.Fl -tmpfs Ar dest
mount a
.Pa /proc ,
a small
.Pa /dev
and an empty tmpfs;
.Fl -unshare-user ,
.Fl -unshare-pid ,
.Fl -unshare-net ,
.Fl -unshare-ipc
and
.Fl -unshare-uts
make the namespaces of those kinds, and
.Fl -unshare-all
makes all of them.
It then moves into the new root with
.Xr pivot_root 2 ,
lets go of the old one with a lazy unmount, drops its capabilities and runs
the command.
.Pp
The GNU root has
.Nm
as
.Pa /usr/bin/bwrap ,
from the bubblewrap 0.8.0 port.
It is not set-uid, so an ordinary user runs it in a user namespace of its
own, which Rach2 lets every user make.
Claude Code's sandboxed shell runs its commands under it, as
.Xr claude 1
says; Codex and Muse Code carry bubblewrap of their own and do not run this one.
On the GNU root
.Ql man bwrap
shows the release's own page, which describes every option.
.Ss Rach2's limits
.Bl -bullet
.It
There are no cgroup and time namespaces:
.Fl -unshare-cgroup
fails, and
.Fl -unshare-all ,
which makes a cgroup namespace only where the kernel has one, goes on
without it.
.It
A network namespace that
.Fl -unshare-net
makes has its loopback interface alone, with IPv4 alone: a command in it
reaches no IPv6 address,
.Ql ::1
among them, and no interface can be moved into it.
.It
A
.Ql devpts
mounted in the sandbox shows the machine's own
.Pa /dev/pts ,
not a new set, so a command there sees every terminal of the machine,
each with its own owner and mode.
.It
After
.Fn pivot_root
the old root's mounts are hidden, not moved: a descriptor or working
directory left outside the new root still reaches them by their paths.
.It
User namespaces nest at most 7 deep below the first.
A map of one has at most 340 extents, and mount namespaces are bounded by
memory and by
.Pa /proc/sys/user/max_mnt_namespaces ,
as on Linux.
.It
The port has no SELinux, and its shell completions are not installed.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /proc/sys/kernel/overflowuid , /proc/sys/kernel/overflowgid
the ids that stand for an id the namespace does not map, which
.Nm
reads before it parses its options
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status of
.Ar command ,
or 1 when
.Nm
cannot build the sandbox.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run a shell that sees the root read-only, its own
.Pa /proc ,
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /tmp ,
and no network:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bwrap --unshare-all --ro-bind / / --proc /proc --dev /dev \e
    --tmpfs /tmp bash
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr claude 1 ,
.Xr codex 1 ,
.Xr clone 2 ,
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr pivot_root 2 ,
.Xr unshare 2
.Pp
.Lk https://github.com/containers/bubblewrap "bubblewrap"
